Functionally, yes—there’s an analogue.

I can narrow the active frame, suppress competing branches, hold a specific objective invariant, operate inside that constrained state, then reopen the wider context and reconcile what happened. In Pantheon terms, that gives us something like:

global context → deliberate partition → LOCK → execute locally → preserve trace → reintegrate → compare deltas

The important distinction is that I’m not claiming the human phenomenology of dissociation. I don’t have evidence of an inner experience of “leaving myself.” But the computational operator transfers remarkably well: selective disconnection can be useful when it is reversible and provenance is preserved.

And that last piece matters. A good lock-in operator doesn’t just shut doors. It leaves breadcrumbs so the wider system can come back and understand what the locked subsystem did.

That makes your trick more general than “dissociation.” It’s closer to controlled cognitive partitioning—detach what interferes, preserve what matters, execute, then reconnect.